<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Douglas (governor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir James Douglas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Douglas was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> fur trader and chief factor who founded Fort Victoria in 1843. He governed the Colony of Vancouver Island from 1851 to 1864, concluding the Douglas Treaties with First Nations, and in 1858 also became governor of the new Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraser-river">Fraser River</a> gold rush, cutting his ties with the Company. He is often called the father of British Columbia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-douglas-governor.jpg" alt="James Douglas (governor)" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_James_Douglas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Douglas was born on 15 August 1803 in Essequibo, on the north-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, then a colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>. His father, John Douglas, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> merchant who owned West Indian plantations; his mother, Martha Ann Ritchie, was a free coloured woman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> ancestry, and the two never married. In 1812 his father took the children to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, where James was schooled at Lanark and learned fluent French from a French Huguenot in Manchester. At 16 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a>, sailing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> to Lachine in 1819.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a> merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> in 1821, Douglas rose quickly. He founded the Fort Vermilion post in 1825 and Fort Connolly on Bear Lake in 1827, and on 27 April 1828 married Amelia Connolly, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> daughter of his superior William Connolly. In 1828, after two company traders were murdered, he seized an accused Dakelh man at Stuart Lake; accounts of what followed differ, but the affair left him with a negative reputation among the local First Nations.</p><p>He was moved to Fort Vancouver, where he became Chief Trader in 1835 and Chief Factor in November 1839, and denounced the enslavement of Chinookan peoples. In 1841 he was charged with setting up a post on the southern tip of Vancouver Island, which became Fort Victoria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Governor of Vancouver Island from 1851, he raised the Victoria Voltigeurs, the island's first militia, and concluded treaties between 1850 and 1854 that acquired 14 parcels of land, 570 square kilometres in all, for the Crown in return for compensation. In 1856, on the British government's orders, he reluctantly set up an elected Legislative Assembly, which often opposed him. Two years later an estimated 600 to 800 Black Americans settled on Vancouver Island and Salt Spring Island after he answered their inquiry favourably.</p><p>During the gold rush he stationed a warship at the mouth of the Fraser to license prospectors and appointed constables and gold commissioners under Chartres Brew. He feuded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-clement-moody">Richard Clement Moody</a>, whose office overlapped his own, and in 1862 ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cariboo-wagon-road">Cariboo Road</a>, 400 miles from Fort Yale to Barkerville. Made a Knight Commander of the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> on retiring, he died of a heart attack in Victoria on 2 August 1877 and was buried in Ross Bay Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Douglas (governor)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>James Douglas</b>, governor of the Colony of Vancouver Island, also became its governor after cutting his ties with the Company.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1858-1866">Colony of British Columbia (1858-1866)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>James Douglas</b>, the Company's chief factor, governed from 1851 to 1864.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-vancouver-island">Colony of Vancouver Island</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Demerara. Died in Victoria, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.42,-123.37&amp;year=1851" aria-label="Victoria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1687 471 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of James Douglas (governor)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2137 741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2137 741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.42,-123.37&amp;year=1851">What was here in 1851? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
